# Feature 15 — Shared Requests Dashboard

## Goal
Give every Lahjah user (regardless of role) visibility into all copy requests
raised by designers. Currently a request raised by a designer is only really
visible to the assigned content writer. This feature adds a shared, org-wide
dashboard that lists every request so anyone with an account can see what's in
flight, who raised it, and where it stands — while keeping actions
(approve / edit / review) restricted to the appropriate roles.

## How it works
- A new dashboard page accessible from the nav to any authenticated user
- On load, fetch all requests from Firestore (not filtered by assignee or owner)
- Display them in a sortable, filterable table
- Everyone can view the table and click into a request's detail page
- The detail page remains read-only for users whose role doesn't permit action;
  action controls (approve, edit, submit review) stay gated by existing role logic
- No new permissions are granted to any role by this feature — it only widens
  *visibility*, not *authority*
- If there are no requests, show a friendly empty state

## Data Structure
No new Firestore collection is required — this reads the existing requests
collection. Each row in the table needs, at minimum:
- `requestNumber` — the request's human-readable ID / number
- `domain` — the domain name the request relates to
- `raisedBy` — the designer who raised it (resolve uid → display name via
  `lib/roles.ts` `getUserDisplayName(uid)`, consistent with existing pages)
- `status` — current status (e.g. pending, in review, approved, revision)
- `createdAt` — timestamp (used for default sort and the "date raised" column)

If any of these fields are not currently stored on a request document, note that
during implementation and confirm before adding new writes — the goal is to read
what already exists wherever possible.

## UI
- New standalone page (e.g. `/dashboard/requests` or `/dashboard/all-requests`)
- Linked from the main nav (`components/DashboardNav.tsx`) for all roles
- A table with columns: Request #, Domain, Raised by, Status, Date raised
- Column headers are clickable to sort (ascending / descending)
- A filter control for status (e.g. dropdown or chips: All / Pending / In review /
  Approved / Revision)
- Optional text filter to search by domain or requester name
- Each row is clickable and links to the existing request detail page
- Status shown as a colored badge for quick scanning
- Default sort: newest first by `createdAt`, but user can re-sort any column
- Responsive layout; table should remain readable on smaller screens
- Loading state while requests are being fetched
- Empty state when there are no requests
- Brand color #FFEA00 for primary actions / active filter state
- Secondary actions #F4F5F6 with #222629 text

## Important Rules for Claude Code
- Always use TypeScript
- Always use Tailwind for styling — no inline styles
- Store all data in Firestore, files in Firebase Storage
- API keys are in .env.local — never hardcode them
- Arabic text must always have dir="rtl" and text-right alignment
- Reuse `getUserDisplayName` from `lib/roles.ts` for showing the requester name
- Do NOT change any role/permission logic — this feature is view-only visibility;
  existing action gating must remain exactly as-is
- After the feature, run `npm run build` to check for errors before committing
- Commit and push to GitHub after the completed feature